# National Income Determination and the Multiplier

> What decides whether an economy produces ₹200 lakh crore this year
> or ₹250? Keynes's answer was deceptively simple: output goes where
> aggregate demand pulls it. The idea of effective demand — and its
> sharpest tool, the multiplier — turned economics from a moral
> science into macroeconomic engineering.

## 1. From Classical to Keynesian Thinking

The classical view held that supply creates its own demand
(**Say's Law**): every act of production pays out exactly enough
income to buy it, so general overproduction is impossible and
unemployment must be temporary — flexible wages would restore full
employment. The Great Depression broke this picture: unemployment
persisted for years, wages fell, and still demand did not return.

[definition]
**Keynes's principle of effective demand**: output and employment in
the short run are determined by **aggregate demand (AD)** — the total
planned spending on goods and services — not by supply alone. If
demand is deficient, the economy can settle at an **underemployment
equilibrium** with no automatic force pulling it to full capacity.
[/definition]

## 2. The Components of Aggregate Demand

@math label="eq:ad-components}
AD = C + I + G + (X - M)
@/math

where $C$ is household consumption, $I$ private investment, $G$
government spending and $(X - M)$ net exports.

**Consumption and the consumption function.** The Keynesian
consumption function is

@math label="eq:consumption-fn}
C = \bar{C} + c\,Y
@/math

where $\bar{C} > 0$ is **autonomous consumption** (people must eat
even at zero income), $c$ is the **marginal propensity to consume
(MPC)** — the fraction of each extra rupee spent — and $s = 1 - c$
is the marginal propensity to save (MPS). The **average propensity
to consume** $C/Y$ falls as income rises, one of Keynes's
"psychological laws".

**Worked example.** If $\bar{C} = 200$ (billion), $c = 0.75$ and
$Y = 2000$: $C = 200 + 1500 = 1700$. Of the last ₹100 of income, ₹75
is spent and ₹25 saved.

## 3. Equilibrium Income: Where Planned Savings Equal Investment

In the simplest two-sector economy (households and firms only),
equilibrium income is where planned investment equals planned
savings:

@math label="eq:equilibrium-y}
Y = \frac{\bar{C} + I}{1 - c}
@/math

**Worked example.** With $\bar{C} = 200$, $c = 0.75$ and planned
investment $I = 100$:

@math label="eq:equilibrium-y-ex}
Y = \frac{200 + 100}{1 - 0.75} = \frac{300}{0.25} = 1200
@/math

At income 1200: consumption $= 200 + 0.75(1200) = 1100$, savings
$= 100$ — exactly matching investment. Below this income, spending
plans exceed output (inventories fall, production rises); above it,
goods pile up unsold (production falls). The economy is pulled toward
1200 like water seeking its level.

## 4. The Investment Multiplier

[definition]
The **multiplier** $k$ is the ratio of the change in equilibrium
income to the change in autonomous spending that caused it:

@math label="eq:multiplier}
k = \frac{\Delta Y}{\Delta I} = \frac{1}{1 - c} = \frac{1}{MPS}
@/math

[/definition]

**Why it happens — the spending chain.** An injection of ₹100 of new
investment becomes ₹100 of someone's income; with MPC 0.75, ₹75 is
re-spent, becoming someone else's income; ₹56.25 of that is spent,
then ₹42.19...

@math label="eq:multiplier-chain}
\Delta Y = 100 \times \left(1 + 0.75 + 0.75^{2} + 0.75^{3} + \cdots\right) = \frac{100}{1 - 0.75} = 400
@/math

[important]
The multiplier works in **both directions** and its size depends only
on the leakages: the higher the MPC, the larger the multiplier. If
MPC = 0.75, $k = 4$; if MPC = 0.5, $k = 2$. Every extra rupee saved,
taxed or spent on imports leaks out of the circular flow and shrinks
the chain — which is why the real-world open-economy multiplier with
taxes is far smaller than the simple textbook value.
[/important]

With proportional taxes at rate $t$ and marginal propensity to
import $m$, the multiplier becomes
$\frac{1}{1 - c(1-t) + m}$ — smaller, because leakages multiply.

## 5. The Paradox of Thrift and Deficient Demand

If every household tries to save more simultaneously (rise in MPS),
aggregate consumption falls, income falls through the multiplier, and
**total savings may end up no higher — or lower**. Individually
rational, collectively self-defeating: the **paradox of thrift**.
This is the intellectual basis for counter-cyclical policy — when
private demand collapses, government spending (or tax cuts) can fill
the gap, riding the same multiplier downward-in-reverse.

## 6. Accelerator, Deflationary Gap and Full Employment

- **Deflationary gap:** the amount by which aggregate demand falls
  short of the level needed for full-employment output. Closing it
  needs $\Delta G = \text{gap} \times (1-c)$.
- **Inflationary gap:** demand exceeds full-employment output;
  output cannot rise, so prices rise instead.
- **Accelerator effect:** rising income induces extra investment to
  expand capacity — investment thus depends on the *rate of change*
  of output, amplifying booms and slumps.

## 7. Limitations and Relevance

The multiplier is a short-run demand-side tool. It assumes spare
capacity and idle labour; near full employment, extra spending feeds
prices, not output. It ignores supply bottlenecks, time lags
(the chain takes quarters, not days), crowding-out (government
borrowing may raise interest rates and displace private investment)
and inflationary expectations. Post-2008 stimulus and COVID-era
relief packages were multiplier logic applied at planetary scale —
with the same debates about debt, capacity and lags that Keynes's
critics raised in 1936.

## 8. Summary

Equilibrium income settles where planned spending equals output:
$Y = (\bar{C} + I)/(1-c)$. The multiplier $1/(1-c)$ turns an
injection of spending into a multiple rise in income through
successive rounds of re-spending, weakened by every leakage (saving,
taxes, imports). Deficient demand can trap an economy below full
employment — and fiscal policy, sized by the multiplier, is the
classic Keynesian response.
